= Cell membrane =

== Overview ==
A '''cell membrane''' is a lipid-and-protein membrane that defines a cell boundary and mediates exchange and communication between the cell and its environment.

== Definition ==
The plasma membrane is built around a phospholipid bilayer with associated proteins. It separates the cytoplasm from the external environment while membrane proteins perform functions such as selective transport, signaling, adhesion, and enzymatic activity.

== Scope & importance ==
The membrane makes cellular organization possible by maintaining different conditions inside and outside the cell while permitting controlled interaction across the boundary.

== Core concepts ==
Core features include the phospholipid bilayer, integral and peripheral membrane proteins, selective permeability, membrane transport, receptors, and dynamic interactions with the cytoskeleton and extracellular environment.

== Examples ==
Ion channels allow selected ions to cross membranes. Receptors bind extracellular signals and trigger intracellular responses. Transport proteins move specific molecules that cannot freely cross the lipid bilayer.

== Applications / implications ==
Membrane biology is central to physiology, neuroscience, pharmacology, microbiology, biotechnology, and the study of cellular energetics and signaling.

== Epistemic notes ==
The 'fluid mosaic' model remains a useful organizing model, but real membranes are heterogeneous, dynamic structures whose composition and organization vary among cells and organelles.

== Transport and chemical gradients ==
The lipid bilayer restricts the spontaneous passage of most ions and large polar solutes, while specific transport proteins allow selective movement. Channels provide pathways for passive flux down an electrochemical gradient; carriers can mediate passive or energy-coupled transport. Pumps and transporters can maintain concentration differences across membranes, using energy directly or indirectly.

Membrane permeability is not all-or-none. It depends on solute properties, membrane composition, transport-protein abundance, voltage, temperature and experimental conditions. A cell with a membrane is not automatically impermeable or isolated from its environment.

== Maintaining cellular conditions ==
[[Related to::Homeostasis|Homeostasis]] is regulation of conditions within viable ranges. Membranes make internal–external gradients possible, but maintaining them also depends on metabolic energy, regulation, environmental inputs and multiple cellular mechanisms. The physical boundary and the physiological control process are distinct concepts.

== Example and model limits ==
Opening an ion channel may allow an electrochemical gradient to drive ionic current. Moving ions changes both chemical composition and electrical potential; the observed effect depends on the ion, channel selectivity and surrounding conditions. The textbook phospholipid-bilayer model is a starting point, not a complete specification of crowded, asymmetric, heterogeneous cellular membranes.

